@schema agentdocs/v1
@id dp3-symmetry-feature-exploration
@title "Symmetry Editing in DeedPlanner 3"
@kind exploration
@description
An exploration of how DeedPlanner 2 implemented symmetry and how DeedPlanner 3 can provide full editing-tool coverage without importing DP2's coupling. It records the chosen interaction, persistence, conflict, tab activation, and keybind behavior, compares two clean implementation scopes, and includes visual directions for the existing Mirror tab.
@end-description

@evaluations
@evaluation metric=size level=3
Full coverage requires migrating every spatial editing path, including height patches, bridges, docks, and future label and border mutations, through one shared boundary.
@end-evaluation
@evaluation metric=complexity level=3
Single-cell, vertex-patch, free-position, and multi-tile aggregate edits must share transformation, per-variant validation, and undo-grouping semantics.
@end-evaluation
@evaluation metric=risk level=3
The feature changes the normal entry point for map mutations, so incomplete migration would produce inconsistent behavior that is difficult for players to predict.
@end-evaluation
@end-evaluations

@decisions
@decision id=D1 status=locked by=human
@question
How should a player place symmetry axes?
@end-question
@selected id=A format=markdown title="Pick directly on the map"
Choose **Vertical**, **Horizontal**, or **Both**, then click a tile center or grid line. A snapped line previews the result before confirmation.
@end-selected
@end-decision

@decision id=D2 status=locked by=human
@question
What should the first release mirror?
@end-question
@selected id=B format=markdown title="All editing tools"
The released feature covers every functional spatial editing tool, including height vertices and multi-tile structures; future spatial tools must enter through the same edit boundary.
@end-selected
@end-decision

@decision id=D3 status=locked by=human
@question
Should axis state be saved in map files?
@end-question
@selected id=A format=markdown title="Session-only"
Axes are an editing aid, reset on map load, and require no save-format migration.
@end-selected
@end-decision

@decision id=D4 status=locked by=human
@question
What should happen when any reflected target is invalid?
@end-question
@selected id=B format=markdown title="Apply valid targets"
Commit the original and every valid reflection while skipping blocked or out-of-map targets.
@end-selected
@end-decision

@decision id=D5 status=locked by=human
@question
How broad should the architectural refactor be?
@end-question
@selected id=A format=markdown title="Typed edit facade"
Route the eleven current editing entry-point files through purpose-named facade methods while keeping existing domain setters and commands intact.
@end-selected
@end-decision
@decision id=D6 status=locked by=human
@question
How should players access symmetry without staying in the Mirror tab?
@end-question
@selected id=A format=markdown title="First-class keybind actions"
Expose symmetry toggles and axis-picking actions through DP3's Input System and Keybinds settings. The Mirror panel and shortcuts invoke the same session state API; keyboard input never owns a parallel symmetry path.
@end-selected
@end-decision
@decision id=D7 status=locked by=human
@question
Which symmetry actions should receive default keyboard bindings?
@end-question
@selected id=A format=markdown title="No defaults"
Expose all five actions in Keybinds but leave them unbound. Power users can choose keys that fit their keyboard, handedness, mouse, and existing bindings.
@end-selected
@end-decision
@decision id=D8 status=locked by=human
@question
How should a map click choose between a tile-center axis and a grid-line axis?
@end-question
@selected id=B format=markdown title="Nearest target"
Snap to whichever tile center or grid line is closest to the pointer and show the result in the live preview before confirmation. An exact tie resolves to the grid line for deterministic placement.
@end-selected
@end-decision
@decision id=D9 status=locked by=human
@question
On a new map with no vertical axis yet, what happens if the player clicks the **Vertical** on/off switch or invokes **Toggle Vertical**?
@end-question
@selected id=B format=markdown title="Require Pick first"
Keep the Vertical switch unavailable until the player clicks the separate **Pick Vertical** control and places the first axis.
@end-selected
@end-decision
@decision id=D10 status=locked by=human
@question
How should the UI report reflected targets skipped by the apply-valid-targets policy?
@end-question
@selected id=C format=markdown title="Silent skip"
Apply every valid variant without an explicit message. Partial results near map edges are normal symmetry behavior and should not produce repetitive notifications.
@end-selected
@end-decision
@end-decisions

@risks
@risk id=R1 kind=edge-case severity=3 tests=T1
@description
Reflecting a wall or border can change which tile owns that edge, causing off-by-one placement or deletion.
@end-description
@end-risk
@risk id=R2 kind=edge-case severity=2 tests=T2
@description
Free-position decorations can land exactly on a tile boundary and need deterministic target-tile normalization and rotation reflection.
@end-description
@end-risk
@risk id=R3 kind=general severity=3 tests=T3
@description
A mirrored stroke could fragment into several undo entries or leave created Unity objects alive on redo disposal.
@end-description
@end-risk
@risk id=R4 kind=general severity=2 tests=T4
@description
Silent clipping must behave consistently for complex bridges, docks, height patches, and decorations so expected edge skips cannot hide an incorrect valid target.
@end-description
@end-risk
@risk id=R5 kind=general severity=1 tests=T5
@description
An axis line can disappear against terrain or be confused with the ordinary tile grid.
@end-description
@end-risk
@risk id=R6 kind=general severity=3 tests=T6
@description
A current or future updater could bypass the spatial-edit boundary and silently stop participating in symmetry.
@end-description
@end-risk
@risk id=R7 kind=general severity=2 tests=T7
@description
User-bound global shortcuts could fire while text entry or another modal UI owns keyboard focus or diverge from the Mirror panel's displayed state.
@end-description
@end-risk
@end-risks

@tests
@test id=T1 tester=unit status=success
@description
Tile centers, vertical edges, horizontal edges, and corners reflect correctly for center-line and grid-line axes at map boundaries.
@end-description
@method
Table-test identity, X, Y, and XY transforms using named target structs; assert coordinates, edge kind, ownership, deduplication, and clipping.
@end-method
@end-test
@test id=T2 tester=unit status=success
@description
Directional ground, floor orientation, decoration position, and decoration rotation retain their visual relationship after reflection.
@end-description
@method
Reflect representative values twice and assert the original value is recovered; include local positions at 0, midpoint, and the upper boundary epsilon.
@end-method
@end-test
@test id=T3 tester=unit status=waiting
@description
One mirrored pointer stroke undoes and redoes as one action without orphaned entities.
@end-description
@method
Submit an edit with both axes active through MapEditFacade, then assert all four locations across execute, undo, redo, and disposal.
@end-method
@end-test
@test id=T4 tester=human status=waiting
@description
Every functional spatial editing tool applies all valid variants and silently skips invalid variants, including frequent partial results near map edges.
@end-description
@method
Exercise ground fill, surface and cave heights, decoration drag/rotation, dock strokes, and bridge create/edit/delete with valid and invalid targets; verify valid variants commit together, invalid variants do not, and no skip notification appears.
@end-method
@end-test
@test id=T5 tester=human status=waiting
@description
Active and preview axes remain legible in 2D, ISO, Wurmian, and 3D camera modes.
@end-description
@method
Inspect cyan preview and gold committed lines over light, dark, cave, and water-heavy maps at near and far zoom levels.
@end-method
@end-test
@test id=T6 tester=unit status=waiting
@description
Editing updaters and presenters cannot introduce direct spatial mutations outside the approved map-edit boundary.
@end-description
@method
Run a source-architecture check that rejects direct Tile mutation and CommandManager submission from editing UI code except inside the map-edit facade and explicit load/import paths.
@end-method
@end-test
@test id=T7 tester=human status=waiting
@description
Mirror controls and user-bound shortcuts stay synchronized, drive the complete workflow from other editing tabs, and do not fire while text or modal input owns focus.
@end-description
@method
Bind every symmetry action in Settings, place both axes, trigger each shortcut from Ground and Height tabs, reset one action to unbound, and repeat while search fields and modal dialogs have focus.
@end-method
@end-test
@end-tests

@section format=markdown title="Recommendation"
Build a **session-only, full-coverage symmetry tool** in the existing Mirror tab. The tab's toggle is currently serialized with `m_Interactable: 0`; enabling that existing toggle is a required part of the feature release, not a separate cleanup. Let the player pick vertical and horizontal axes directly on the map, keep each axis independently enabled, and render persistent axis lines even after leaving the tab.

The released feature should mirror every functional spatial edit, including surface/cave heights, bridges, docks, fills, and continuous object manipulation. Border and label tabs are currently stubs; when their editing behavior is implemented, it must use the same spatial-edit boundary from its first commit. Directional content should always transform correctly; DP2's optional **Mirror Floors** toggle should not return because producing the wrong orientation is not a useful mode.

This is not a safe bolt-on to the existing setters, but it does not require rewriting the whole editing architecture. Add one typed map-edit facade in front of the eleven current mutation entry-point files and keep the existing domain setters, factories, commands, and `CommandManager`. When both axes are active, the facade expands one call to identity, X, Y, and XY variants, skips invalid variants, and commits every applied variant as one undoable action.

Add rebindable **Toggle Vertical**, **Toggle Horizontal**, **Pick Vertical**, **Pick Horizontal**, and **Clear Symmetry** input actions. They remain usable from ordinary editing tabs and call the same behavior used by the Mirror panel, so keybind support adds one input adapter rather than a second implementation. The current recommendation is to expose them unbound rather than ship an awkward universal layout.
@end-section

@section format=markdown title="Keybind Feasibility"
Binding the full workflow is feasible without spreading symmetry through the input architecture:

- `InputSettings` already enables the generated input asset globally, and `KeybindSettingsRegistrar` automatically exposes every non-UI action in Settings with persisted overrides and reset support.
- The installed Input System supports `TwoModifiers` composites. A live Editor probe successfully created and displayed a `Control+Shift+X` binding, so deliberate multi-modifier defaults are technically supported.
- Put the five actions in a dedicated **Symmetry** action map. One always-active `SymmetryShortcuts` adapter subscribes to them and invokes the same session-state behavior as the Mirror presenter.
- A Pick shortcut stores the current tab, switches `TabContext` to Mirror for the map click, and restores the previous tab after placement or cancellation. Existing tab coordination therefore prevents the prior editing updater from consuming the same click.
- The adapter must ignore shortcuts while a text input or modal window owns focus. DP3 already applies a similar focus rule to camera input, but there is no shared global shortcut guard today, so this is a small required addition rather than free existing behavior.

No default scheme tested conceptually is a strong fit:

- Multi-modifier chords avoid conflicts but are uncomfortable with one hand on the mouse.
- Bare number keys are reachable but semantically arbitrary and consume a prominent shortcut row.
- A `Z`–`B` left-hand cluster is fast on QWERTY but arbitrary and changes physical shape on layouts such as QWERTZ.
- Function keys conflict with browser, screenshot, and existing DP3 behavior.
- Extra mouse buttons are ergonomic but not universally available.
- A leader-key or temporary shortcut layer would solve namespace pressure by adding interaction and implementation complexity disproportionate to this feature.

Leaving the actions unbound is therefore a deliberate default, not incomplete keybind support. They should appear together under **Symmetry** in Settings, with the Mirror panel showing **Unbound** beside each action and offering a direct route to Keybinds. Players who want the power-user workflow can bind a personal left-hand cluster or mouse buttons without DP3 guessing their hardware and posture.

**Cancel axis pick** does not need a sixth symmetry action; the existing UI Cancel/Escape behavior should cancel placement and restore the previous tab.
@end-section

