@end-section

@section format=markdown title="What DP2 Actually Does"
DP2 keeps one `Symmetry` object on the map plus three global toggles: X enabled, Y enabled, and whether floor orientation is reflected. Its editor requires a selected tile, then supports:

- an axis through that tile's center;
- an axis along one of its four borders;
- two axes through one of its four corners;
- independent on/off toggles without discarding the stored coordinates;
- clearing all axis state.

The coordinate math emits the original edit plus an X copy, a Y copy, and an XY copy. Separate mutation methods account for road diagonals, floor orientation, horizontal/vertical wall ownership, borders, object slots, and object rotation. The individual DP2 updaters call those methods after mutating the source, so symmetry is duplicated across tool code and bypasses a unified undo transaction.

Coverage in DP2 is ground, ordinary floors, roofs, walls/fences, borders, caves, objects, animals, and object rotation. Searches of the final repository show no symmetry calls from height, bridge, or label editing. The axis visualization is a pulsing tile fill for tile-centered locks or dotted lines for border/corner locks.
@end-section

@section format=markdown title="The Architectural Elephant"
DP2 works because every updater remembers to make a second call into `Symmetry`: ground calls `mirrorGround`, walls call `mirrorHorizontalWall` or `mirrorVerticalWall`, objects call `mirrorObject`, and so on. That is precisely the pattern DP3 should avoid. It makes symmetry a cross-cutting branch inside every tool, duplicates coordinate rules, and lets new edit paths silently omit the feature.

There is no clean zero-refactor hook in current DP3:

| Tempting hook | Why it fails |
|---|---|
| `CommandManager` interceptor | Commands are opaque and frequently private; floors, walls, decorations, bridges, and docks often create Unity objects before command submission, so reflection here would clone partially constructed state and redo could expand it again. |
| `Tile.Set*` methods | Domain objects would depend on transient editing mode, loaders/importers could mirror accidentally, and recursion-suppression flags would spread through the model. |
| `CommandManager.Mutated` observer | The event fires after mutation and carries no semantic or spatial information; mirroring would become a second history operation. |
| Calls added to every updater | This recreates DP2's architecture and guarantees coverage drift. |

Full coverage therefore changes the prerequisite: establish one spatial-edit application boundary first. Symmetry then becomes one policy at that boundary rather than a feature injected into every domain type. The boundary can be narrow and typed; a universal edit-object framework is optional, not required.
@end-section

@section format=markdown title="Recommended Lean Boundary"
The recommended D5-A flow is:

`Updater / presenter → purpose-named MapEditFacade method → symmetry targets → existing setter/factory/command`

Only `MapEditFacade` and its private symmetry collaborators read `SymmetryState`. Updaters know that they are requesting `SetFloor`, `ApplyHeightPatch`, `PlaceBridge`, or `PaintDockPath`, not whether symmetry is enabled. Domain entities and commands remain symmetry-blind and do not need replacement.

| Piece | Responsibility |
|---|---|
| `SymmetryState` | Session-only axes and enablement; no Unity objects and no map serialization. |
| `GridReflection` | A named value describing identity, X, Y, or XY reflection around doubled-coordinate axes. |
| `SymmetryGeometry` | Pure transforms for cells, vertices, edges, poses, spans, paths, orientations, and rotations. |
| `MapEditFacade` | Purpose-named user edit methods; expands targets, deduplicates, skips invalid variants, delegates to existing mutations, and retains one undo action. |
| Aggregate helpers | Private bridge, dock, height-patch, and decoration routines where transformation needs domain-specific validation or ordering. |

This requires one migration per edit entry point, but each is normally a dependency addition plus replacement of a direct mutation call. Save/load, import, map repair, and debug generation remain explicit non-user paths that bypass the facade.

The broader D5-B intent pipeline remains a valid future refactor, but symmetry does not justify forcing every edit into a new object hierarchy now. If another feature later needs arbitrary rotation, copy transforms, macros, or network replication, the typed facade provides a natural place to introduce intents incrementally.
@end-section

@section format=markdown title="Transform Model"
Represent each axis as a doubled tile coordinate (`AxisCoordinate2`). Even values describe grid lines; odd values describe tile centers. For a cell at `x`, reflection is `x' = AxisCoordinate2 - x - 1`; vertices and edges use their own named value transforms, avoiding DP2's direction-specific ownership adjustments.

Full coverage falls into four reusable shapes:

| Shape | Editing examples | Transformation rule |
|---|---|---|
| Cell / edge | Ground, floors, roofs, walls, fences, borders, cave terrain | Transform cell or edge, then transform directional data such as diagonal, orientation, and reversal. |
| Pose | Decorations and labels | Reflect world position and heading, normalize back to a target cell/local position, retain the asset without mesh mirroring. |
| Vertex patch | Surface heights, cave floor, cave clearance, ramps, flatten/paint modes | Build the complete set of vertex target values first, transform the patch, deduplicate overlaps, then commit one patch command. |
| Aggregate / path | Bridges, docks, fill regions, drag strokes | Transform endpoints or the complete ordered path, reverse order where handedness changes, validate the entire aggregate, then create domain objects. |

The important rule is that symmetry transforms the **user's semantic operation**, not the resulting object graph. That preserves intent for bridges, docks, and height tools instead of trying to infer it after mutation.
@end-section

@section format=markdown title="Coverage Without Leakage"
The feature can cover everything without becoming omnipresent if the boundary is enforced:

- Editing UI code calls purpose-named `MapEditFacade` behavior and never calls `Tile.Set*` or `CommandManager.AddToActionAndExecute` directly.
- `MapEditFacade` is the only editing-layer owner of symmetry expansion and action rollback.
- The existing `CommandManager` remains responsible only for execute/undo/redo and object disposal.
- A source audit keeps direct spatial mutation calls out of updater and presenter folders; the remaining direct history access is limited to undo/redo and bridge paving's live-preview suspension.
- Each new spatial tool adds one facade behavior and keeps any required transformation semantics inside the edit module rather than the UI presenter.
- Non-spatial commands—save, load, camera, visibility, settings, undo, and redo—never enter the facade.

`MirrorUpdater` remains narrow: it presents axis state, performs map picking, and controls preview/committed axis visuals. It does not dispatch mirrored map edits.

`SymmetryShortcuts` is one always-active input adapter subscribed to a dedicated `DPInput.Symmetry` action map. It calls the same purpose-named state behavior as the Mirror presenter and suppresses actions while text or modal input owns focus. Because `KeybindSettingsRegistrar` automatically enumerates every non-UI input action, the new actions appear in Settings without a symmetry-specific rebinding screen.
@end-section

@section format=markdown title="Expected Change Scope"
The repository currently has **eleven user-editing entry-point files** that directly mutate spatial state: seven primary updaters, `BridgesUpdater`, and three bridge presenters. Docks already live inside `FloorUpdater`; Border and Label updaters are currently stubs.

For the recommended typed-facade approach, the expected order of magnitude is:

| Area | Existing files modified | New files | Approximate code |
|---|---:|---:|---:|
| Symmetry state, geometry, facade, DI | 1–2 | 4–6 | 450–750 lines |
| Mirror panel, tab activation, axis visualization, and input actions | 4–6 | 3–4 | 350–600 lines |
| Ground/floor/wall/roof/cave/object integration | 6 | 0–2 | 250–450 lines changed |
| Height, dock, and bridge integration | 5 | 1–2 | 450–800 lines changed |
| EditMode and interaction tests | 1–2 | 5–7 | 700–1,200 lines |
| Scene map, changelog, and supporting docs | 2–3 | 0 | Small targeted edits |
| **Likely total** | **19–24** | **13–19 authored files** | **About 2,200–3,800 lines including tests** |

The uncertainty is concentrated in mirrored bridge editing/paving and live height previews, not in ordinary tile editing. A discovery spike on those two paths should narrow the estimate before implementation.

What should **not** be rewritten: `CommandManager`, tile/entity command implementations, serialization, database catalogs, cameras, map loading, and most domain factories. Bridge and dock factories should be reused; at most they need small behavior APIs for locating a reflected aggregate.

For comparison:

| Approach | Files touched | Approximate code | Character |
|---|---:|---:|---|
| D5-A · Typed facade | 32–43 meaningful files; roughly 45–62 Git entries with Unity `.meta` files | 2,200–3,800 lines | Recommended; substantial feature, targeted refactor |
| D5-B · General intent pipeline | 40–55 meaningful files | 3,500–5,500 lines | Stronger platform architecture, but disproportionate for symmetry alone |
| D5-C · DP2-style calls | 18–24 meaningful files | 1,200–2,200 lines | Smaller initial diff, highest long-term coupling and omission risk |

So the feature is large because D2 includes heights, bridges, and docks, but the clean implementation need not be a whole-program rewrite. Most changed production files receive a narrow call-site substitution; the bulk of new code is isolated geometry and verification.
@end-section

@section format=markdown title="Delivery Phasing"
D2 requires every functional spatial tool in the released feature, but the implementation should still be built behind the inactive Mirror tab in dependency order:

| Internal phase | Deliverable | Gate |
|---|---|---|
| 1 · Foundation | Doubled-coordinate reflections, `SymmetryGeometry`, typed `MapEditFacade`, per-target validation | Pure EditMode geometry and transaction tests pass |
| 2 · Cell and pose edits | Ground/fill, floors, roofs, walls/fences, decorations, cave terrain | Existing editing behavior unchanged with symmetry disabled |
| 3 · Vertex patches | Every surface and cave height mode, including drag previews, ramps, flattening, and cancellation | Mirrored live edit undoes/redoes as one action |
| 4 · Aggregates | Bridge create/edit/delete/paving and dock paint/erase/support direction | Each variant validates before its Unity object is created; invalid variants are skipped silently |
| 5 · UI and release | Axis picker, persistent visuals, live axis preview, input actions, architecture lint, full interaction matrix | Flip the existing Mirror toggle from non-interactable to interactable only after every functional spatial tool and shortcut test passes |

Border and Label tabs currently contain no mutation behavior. Their eventual implementations must start on `MapEditFacade`; they do not justify delaying symmetry for functionality that does not yet exist.
@end-section

@section format=markdown title="Implementation Status"
The approved design is implemented in the working tree. A session-scoped `SymmetrySession` and typed `MapEditFacade` now own reflection for ground, cave terrain, surface and cave heights, floors, roofs, walls, decorations, docks, and bridge creation/editing/deletion/paving. Height patches are merged before mutation so direct vertices win over overlapping reflections. Dock paint and bridge-paving strokes capture symmetry once per gesture, and bridge placement validates its complete reflected batch before constructing accepted variants. Reflected targets are validated independently and share the initiating undo action.

The existing Mirror tab is enabled and contains independent axis controls, automatic center/grid-line snapping, a live pick preview, persistent committed map guides, and clear/cancel behavior. The panel lists all five shortcut bindings and opens the existing Settings window directly. The generated Input System actions have empty defaults, so both the panel and Settings render them as **Unbound**. Shortcut axis picking temporarily enters Mirror and returns to the prior tab, while focused text fields and modal windows suppress global symmetry actions without treating the always-on tooltip as a modal.

Compilation is clean and all **242 EditMode tests pass**, including transform, clipping, and overlapping-height-patch coverage. The real MainScene flow verified the rendered panel, map-click axis placement, off-map Escape cancellation, toggle/clear controls, direct Settings route, temporary user binding, return-to-previous-tab behavior, and modal shortcut suppression. On SteamOS, automated Play mode must launch the Editor with `-noaudio`; without it Unity 6000.3.20f1 crashes in its FMOD audio thread before the Pipeline reconnects.
@end-section

@section format=markdown title="Sources and Evidence"
- [DP2 symmetry editor](https://github.com/Warlander/DeedPlanner-2/blob/d430582cf38fd49fe2eefb7d9e02074eb98b0237/src/main/java/pl/wurmonline/deedplanner/forms/SymmetryEditor.java) — UI state, center/border/corner locking, independent axis toggles.
- [DP2 symmetry engine](https://github.com/Warlander/DeedPlanner-2/blob/d430582cf38fd49fe2eefb7d9e02074eb98b0237/src/main/java/pl/wurmonline/deedplanner/logic/symmetry/Symmetry.java) — coordinate, orientation, wall/border, object-position, and rotation transforms.
- [DP2 ground integration](https://github.com/Warlander/DeedPlanner-2/blob/d430582cf38fd49fe2eefb7d9e02074eb98b0237/src/main/java/pl/wurmonline/deedplanner/logic/ground/GroundUpdater.java) and [wall integration](https://github.com/Warlander/DeedPlanner-2/blob/d430582cf38fd49fe2eefb7d9e02074eb98b0237/src/main/java/pl/wurmonline/deedplanner/logic/walls/WallUpdater.java) — source edit followed by explicit symmetry calls.
- [DP2 release history](https://forum.wurmonline.com/index.php?/topic/79352-deedplanner-2910-3d-house-and-deed-planner/) — symmetry shipped in 2.4.0 and the editor was later centered in 2.8.9.
- [DP3 MirrorUpdater](https://github.com/Warlander/DeedPlanner-3/blob/41ee15a429bb702ed886132216febf2b02334332/Assets/Warlander/Deedplanner/Editing/Mirror/MirrorUpdater.cs), [Mirror tab prefab](https://github.com/Warlander/DeedPlanner-3/blob/41ee15a429bb702ed886132216febf2b02334332/Assets/Prefabs/MainScene/Tabs/Mirror%20Tab.prefab), and [composition wiring](https://github.com/Warlander/DeedPlanner-3/blob/41ee15a429bb702ed886132216febf2b02334332/Assets/Warlander/Deedplanner/Composition/MainSceneLifetimeScope.cs) — existing DP3 feature seam.
- [DP3 CommandManager](https://github.com/Warlander/DeedPlanner-3/blob/41ee15a429bb702ed886132216febf2b02334332/Assets/Warlander/Deedplanner/Editing/CommandManager.cs) and [Tile mutation APIs](https://github.com/Warlander/DeedPlanner-3/blob/41ee15a429bb702ed886132216febf2b02334332/Assets/Warlander/Deedplanner/Domain/Tile.cs) — action grouping and reversible entity changes.

The mockups match the verified DP3 side-panel structure (tab grid → level chooser → active tab) and existing parchment/wood control language. The implementation enables the formerly non-interactable Mirror toggle, and a live 1280×720 composite capture confirmed the new panel is active and laid out correctly in MainScene.
@end-section
